1
00:00:05,370 --> 00:00:11,493
Elizabeth Bennet and Mr Darcy almost don't get together, because they judge each other too fast.

2
00:00:11,799 --> 00:00:16,998
This guide covers the novel and a version where a rainy week gives them time to talk.

3
00:00:18,225 --> 00:00:22,183
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

4
00:00:22,497 --> 00:00:26,986
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through Pride and Prejudice, by Jane Austen.

5
00:00:27,199 --> 00:00:31,740
It's a quick guide you can use for class, a book club, or your own curiosity.

6
00:00:31,972 --> 00:00:36,035
The full guide, with ten museum and archive sources, is linked below.

7
00:00:38,247 --> 00:00:45,457
Mrs Bennet has five daughters and no sons, so the family home will go to a cousin when Mr Bennet dies.

8
00:00:45,722 --> 00:00:48,496
She badly wants her girls to marry well.

9
00:00:48,954 --> 00:00:55,289
When a rich young man, Mr Bingley, rents Netherfield Hall nearby, she gets very excited.

10
00:00:56,029 --> 00:00:58,964
Bingley falls for the eldest daughter, Jane.

11
00:00:59,257 --> 00:01:06,388
His proud friend Mr Darcy insults the second daughter, Elizabeth, at a ball, and she decides she dislikes him.

12
00:01:06,683 --> 00:01:12,808
A charming officer, Mr Wickham, tells her a false story about Darcy, and she believes it.

13
00:01:13,059 --> 00:01:17,181
Darcy proposes, and Elizabeth turns him down angrily.

14
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:20,850
He writes her a letter explaining the truth about Wickham.

15
00:01:21,149 --> 00:01:29,603
Slowly each of them sees their own pride and prejudice, and when Darcy quietly saves her family from scandal, she realises she

16
00:01:29,603 --> 00:01:30,524
loves him.

17
00:01:31,149 --> 00:01:34,812
Next, who's who, so you can keep everyone straight.

18
00:01:36,743 --> 00:01:37,968
Elizabeth Bennet.

19
00:01:38,011 --> 00:01:39,771
Clever and quick to judge.

20
00:01:39,866 --> 00:01:42,038
She's the second of five sisters.

21
00:01:42,772 --> 00:01:44,355
Fitzwilliam Darcy.

22
00:01:44,548 --> 00:01:49,007
A rich, proud, shy man who's bad at talking to strangers.

23
00:01:49,576 --> 00:01:50,706
Jane Bennet.

24
00:01:50,862 --> 00:01:53,797
The eldest sister, gentle and kind.

25
00:01:54,764 --> 00:01:56,070
Charles Bingley.

26
00:01:56,383 --> 00:01:59,596
Darcy's cheerful friend, who rents Netherfield.

27
00:01:59,906 --> 00:02:01,254
Caroline Bingley.

28
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:04,791
Bingley's sister, who wants to marry Darcy herself.

29
00:02:05,695 --> 00:02:06,927
Mrs Bennet.

30
00:02:07,115 --> 00:02:10,407
The girls' mother, who's anxious to see them married.

31
00:02:12,537 --> 00:02:14,556
Now for the story.

32
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:17,876
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

33
00:02:18,305 --> 00:02:19,407
Chapter one.

34
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:21,263
Three Miles of Mud.

35
00:02:22,079 --> 00:02:27,194
Like so many things in that family, it began with one of Mrs Bennet's schemes.

36
00:02:27,774 --> 00:02:34,933
Jane, the eldest of the five Bennet sisters, had been invited to dine at Netherfield with Mr Bingley's sisters.

37
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:41,284
Mrs Bennet wanted Jane to marry Mr Bingley very much, so she wouldn't let her take the carriage.

38
00:02:41,649 --> 00:02:45,556
Jane must go on horseback, she said, because it looked like rain.

39
00:02:45,687 --> 00:02:48,601
And if it rained, Jane would have to stay the night.

40
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:51,660
It did rain, all evening.

41
00:02:51,925 --> 00:02:55,008
Jane had ridden there in it and caught a bad cold.

42
00:02:55,311 --> 00:03:00,168
The next morning a note came from Netherfield to say she was too unwell to come home.

43
00:03:00,471 --> 00:03:04,635
Her sister Elizabeth couldn't bear to sit at home while Jane was ill.

44
00:03:04,988 --> 00:03:08,500
There was no carriage to be had, and Elizabeth couldn't ride.

45
00:03:08,656 --> 00:03:12,836
So she did what she always did when something mattered, and she walked.

46
00:03:13,414 --> 00:03:14,512
Chapter two.

47
00:03:14,702 --> 00:03:15,761
The Library.

48
00:03:16,630 --> 00:03:20,589
Netherfield had a library, though not a very good one.

49
00:03:21,242 --> 00:03:26,096
Mr Bingley had only lately taken the house, and he wasn't much of a reader.

50
00:03:26,433 --> 00:03:30,185
He cheerfully admitted he had more books than he ever looked into.

51
00:03:30,536 --> 00:03:34,890
The shelves were half empty, and most of the books there had come with the house.

52
00:03:35,268 --> 00:03:41,006
There were old sermons, old histories, and a few books of poems nobody had ever opened.

53
00:03:41,301 --> 00:03:43,601
But it was a quiet room with a good fire.

54
00:03:44,195 --> 00:03:50,069
On the fourth wet afternoon, Elizabeth found her way into it, looking for something to read to Jane.

55
00:03:50,835 --> 00:03:53,132
Mr Darcy was already there.

56
00:03:53,999 --> 00:03:55,154
Chapter three.

57
00:03:55,318 --> 00:03:56,235
The Letter.

58
00:03:56,663 --> 00:04:01,296
After that afternoon, the evenings at Netherfield were a little different.

59
00:04:01,663 --> 00:04:03,654
In most ways they were the same.

60
00:04:03,908 --> 00:04:07,165
Mr Bingley still talked cheerfully about everything.

61
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:13,585
His sister Mrs Hurst still played cards with her husband, who still fell asleep on the sofa after dinner.

62
00:04:13,803 --> 00:04:21,772
And Caroline Bingley, who wanted to marry Mr Darcy herself, still sat as near him as she could and praised everything he did.

63
00:04:22,657 --> 00:04:25,123
There's a famous evening like this in the book.

64
00:04:25,322 --> 00:04:31,597
Mr Darcy is writing a letter to his young sister Georgiana, and Miss Bingley watches every line.

65
00:04:31,861 --> 00:04:35,741
She praises how even his writing is and how long his letters are.

66
00:04:36,005 --> 00:04:41,210
She asks him to tell his sister she longs to see her, until he can hardly write at all.

67
00:04:41,487 --> 00:04:46,110
Elizabeth sits with her needlework, quietly and wickedly amused.

68
00:04:46,459 --> 00:04:47,854
The Last Page.

69
00:04:48,614 --> 00:04:50,711
The rain stopped on a Sunday.

70
00:04:51,373 --> 00:04:56,006
Elizabeth woke to an odd silence and lay wondering about it for a while.

71
00:04:56,383 --> 00:04:59,759
Then she realised it was the missing sound of rain on the roof.

72
00:05:00,224 --> 00:05:01,612
She went to the window.

73
00:05:01,817 --> 00:05:08,418
The garden was washed and shining, the sky was a clean pale blue, and there were birds everywhere.

74
00:05:08,845 --> 00:05:12,387
Jane was well enough to travel, so the carriage was ordered.

75
00:05:12,683 --> 00:05:18,126
By the middle of the morning the two sisters were standing in the hall in their bonnets, saying goodbye.

76
00:05:18,858 --> 00:05:22,836
Miss Bingley was very polite, and very glad to see them go.

77
00:05:23,170 --> 00:05:26,743
Mr Bingley was sorry and said so many times.

78
00:05:26,908 --> 00:05:30,184
As usual, Mr Darcy said almost nothing.

79
00:05:30,506 --> 00:05:36,797
But when he handed Elizabeth into the carriage, he held her hand a moment longer than he strictly needed to.

80
00:05:37,062 --> 00:05:40,813
And he said quietly, "I hope your sister continues well."

81
00:05:41,434 --> 00:05:45,542
Next, the big ideas, the ones that come up in class.

82
00:05:47,699 --> 00:05:49,136
First impressions.

83
00:05:49,321 --> 00:05:53,009
Austen's first title for the book was First Impressions.

84
00:05:53,185 --> 00:05:55,850
Both main characters judge too quickly.

85
00:05:56,211 --> 00:05:57,808
Pride and prejudice.

86
00:05:58,072 --> 00:06:01,991
Darcy's pride and Elizabeth's prejudice both get in the way.

87
00:06:02,295 --> 00:06:03,951
Each has a bit of both.

88
00:06:04,650 --> 00:06:06,149
Marriage and money.

89
00:06:06,541 --> 00:06:12,765
Women in 1811 often couldn't inherit property, so marriage decided a woman's future.

90
00:06:13,171 --> 00:06:14,732
Time to know someone.

91
00:06:15,149 --> 00:06:20,756
Our version suggests that people often change their minds when they simply have more time together.

92
00:06:21,376 --> 00:06:25,206
Next, the exact moment our version changes the book.

93
00:06:27,617 --> 00:06:29,161
So what's the what-if?

94
00:06:29,203 --> 00:06:31,325
Here's the turning point we changed.

95
00:06:32,113 --> 00:06:37,763
In the book, Jane falls ill at Netherfield and Elizabeth stays a few days to nurse her.

96
00:06:37,998 --> 00:06:42,306
In our story the rain doesn't stop, and they stay more than a week.

97
00:06:42,669 --> 00:06:48,678
Everything after that moment is new, but the characters still act the way their authors wrote them.

98
00:06:50,711 --> 00:06:54,060
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

99
00:06:54,727 --> 00:07:00,801
Jane Austen was born in 1775, the daughter of a country vicar in Hampshire.

100
00:07:01,185 --> 00:07:06,513
She wrote the first version of this book in her early twenties and called it First Impressions.

101
00:07:06,909 --> 00:07:09,889
It was rejected, and she revised it for years.

102
00:07:10,254 --> 00:07:13,828
When it came out in 1813, her name wasn't on it.

103
00:07:14,021 --> 00:07:18,489
The title page said only "By the Author of Sense and Sensibility."

104
00:07:20,581 --> 00:07:26,031
The book is set during the Regency, when wealthy families lived on income from land.

105
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:30,349
Most women couldn't own much property or work in good jobs.

106
00:07:30,691 --> 00:07:36,767
A good marriage could decide a woman's whole future, and that's why Mrs Bennet worries so much.

107
00:07:38,876 --> 00:07:40,350
Want to see the real thing?

